# Template for the three-arm comparison's real policy artifact (AAASM-5805).
#
# Identical to confined-arm.yaml.tmpl (AAASM-5713) with one deliberate
# omission: no `network:` node.
#
# Why: `aa-isolation-native/src/capability.rs` reports NetworkEgress as
# SupportLevel::Unsupported. Stating `network.allowlist` turns
# `lower_policy`'s output into a `Required` NetworkEgress requirement
# (`LoweringOptions::strict()`), and `aa-isolation/src/plan.rs`'s
# `negotiate` turns an unsupported domain + a Required posture into a
# PlanRefusal before any process starts — every family in the native arm
# would refuse identically at plan time. A same-policy three-arm comparison
# therefore cannot state a network grant at all; the native arm has to be
# measured under a policy it can actually run.
#
# Consequence, stated plainly rather than left for a reader to notice:
# `https_loopback` runs UNCONFINED IN THE EGRESS DOMAIN IN EVERY ARM under
# this policy, so its P5 figure is ~1.0 by construction in all three arms
# and must not be read as "egress confinement is free" — the same caveat
# confined-arm.yaml.tmpl already states for P4/process-spawn, restated here
# because this template drops the one thing that would have made it
# otherwise true for the sandlock/unconfined pair. Symmetrically, no
# `syscall_allowlist` is stated either, so native's seccomp cost is not in
# these numbers.
#
# See ../policy/README.md and ./confined-arm.yaml.tmpl for the filesystem
# grants below, which are unchanged from the two-arm comparison.
apiVersion: agent-assembly/v1
kind: Policy
metadata:
  name: aabench-three-arm
spec:
  tools:
    "*":
      allow: true
  filesystem:
    read:
      allow:
        - "/"
    write:
      allow:
        - "__SCRATCH_ROOT__"
        - "/dev/null"
